.TH DIFTRAYREMAP 1
.SH NAME
diftrayremap \- system-wide key remapping for DiftrayWM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diftrayremap check
.RI [ KEYMAP ]
.br
.B diftrayremap list
.RI [ KEYMAP ]
.br
.B diftrayremap run
.RI [ KEYMAP ]
.br
.B diftrayremap
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
diftrayremap is the system-wide half of DiftrayWM's key remapper. The compositor
applies the keymap INI to the keys it already reads, unprivileged and scoped to
its own session. This tool does the other half: it takes the physical keyboard
with EVIOCGRAB and republishes it through uinput with the same profile applied.
Because the events are transformed underneath every consumer, the remapping
applies to the whole machine — the compositor, a TTY, another session, or
anything reading evdev directly.
.PP
It reads the same keymap.ini the compositor does, so the mapping is defined
once. See
.BR diftraywm\-keymap (5)
for the file format.
.SH WHY A SEPARATE PROGRAM
That reach is why diftrayremap is a separate binary rather than a mode of the
compositor.
.PP
The compositor must not need root. A compositor that grabbed the physical
keyboard would take it away from whatever session it is nested inside, and
running a window manager as root would be a far worse trade than not having
system-wide remapping. Splitting the two lets the common case stay
unprivileged and keeps the privileged, machine-wide behaviour a deliberate,
separate decision.
.SH PRIVILEGES
Opening /dev/input/event* and /dev/uinput normally requires either root or a
udev rule granting access. Both nodes are mode 0660 root-owned by default, and
/dev/input/event* is additionally group
.BR input .
.PP
Either run the tool as root, or add a rule granting the narrow access it needs.
A rule of this shape is sufficient:
.PP
.RS
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", GROUP="input", MODE="0660"
KERNEL=="event*", SUBSYSTEM=="input", GROUP="input", MODE="0660"
.RE
.PP
together with adding the user or service that runs diftrayremap to the
.B input
group. Do not grant 0666.
.PP
.B check
reports what is missing and prints the exact remedy, so there is no need to
guess.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Report whether remapping can run, and what it found. Prints whether
/dev/uinput exists and is writable, whether the effective user is root, whether
it is in the
.B input
group, the keymap's summary, and the keyboards
.B [devices]
resolves to. Ends with a verdict line: ready, nothing to remap, or unavailable
with the reason. Exits 0 only when it is ready.
.TP
.B list
Print the keyboards the keymap's
.B [devices]
section resolves to, as
.IR VENDOR:PRODUCT ,
device node and name — one per line. Use it to fill in
.BR [devices] ,
so the identifiers do not have to be guessed. Exits 1 when nothing matches.
.TP
.B run
Claim the matching keyboards, publish the virtual device, and remap until
interrupted. This is the usual mode.
.B SIGINT
.RB ( Ctrl-C ),
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGHUP
all stop it cleanly: every grab is released and the virtual device is destroyed,
so the physical keyboard works again immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The evdev layer can only express what is meaningful at the device level:
.BR Ignore() ,
.B Remap()
and
.B Trigger()
work there.
.BR Diftray() ,
.B Exec()
and
.B Typeout()
are compositor actions with no evdev equivalent, so those keys pass through
untouched and the compositor's own copy of the keymap handles them.
.PP
One virtual device is published, so
.B [devices]
may name only a single keyboard. A keymap naming several is refused with an
explanation rather than half-applied.
.PP
The tool also refuses to run when
.B system_wide
is not requested, when no keyboard in
.B [devices]
is connected, and when the connected keyboard is not the one named. Each of
those is reported as a specific message.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DIFTRAYWM_KEYMAP
The file to read when no argument is given. Defaults to
.BR keymap.ini .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The command was refused or failed. The reason is on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find out what the machine can do, and what is plugged in:
.PP
.RS
diftrayremap check keymap.ini
diftrayremap list keymap.ini
.RE
.PP
Fill in
.B [devices]
from that output, then start remapping:
.PP
.RS
diftrayremap run keymap.ini
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
diftraywm\-keymap(5), diftrayctl(1)
